WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:00.630 align:start position:0%
 
i think there might be general agreement  that it is somewhat difficult to present  a clear account  of four years of work upon a  controversial topic  a new surgical approach to the treatment  of mental illness in 10 minutes  in an attempt to accomplish this task i  would like to read an abstract from a  paper scheduled for presentation of the  harvey cushing society  at its annual meeting in st louis  missouri on april the 19th 1966  i will then illustrate its content and  add as much to it as time allows  the abstract will read as follows  the beneficial effects of interruption  of cingulate fibers upon certain  psychiatric illnesses  have been well documented over the last  twenty years by cairns lewin poole and  lebeau among others  these surgeons have however preferred a  direct attack on the singulum  in 1961 fultz and white presented to  this society that is the harvey cushing  society  a series of 16 patients suffering from  intractable pain in whom lesions were  placed in the cingulum by stereotactic  electrode placement for the relief of  pain using a slight modification of  their technique we began in 1962 an  investigation of this procedure for the  treatment of mental illness primarily  manic depressive psychosis  to date 51 psychiatric patients have  been operated upon 75 times  10 have had cingulotomy performed for  the intractable pain of incurable cancer  there have been no operative or  post-operative complications no patients  have to date suffered undesirable  alterations personality or intelligence  thirty six of forty psychiatric patients  followed three months to four years  postoperatively  have shown varying degrees of  improvement  and in thirty this has been useful  improvement  seven of the ten cancer patients have  had satisfactory pain relief  that's the end of the abstract now if we  may have the illustrative material  we have the first slide please  this is the area under attack the limbic  system  more specifically the cingulum  this fiber bundle the cingulum bundle  seems to be the main link between the  frontal lobes and the limbic lobe the  latter is thought to be in animals at  least a prime regulator of behavior and  emotion  if i may be permitted to use the term  emotion in respect to animals  may we have the next slide please  a word about technique  bilateral burr holes are placed 9.5  centimeters posterior to the nasion  and 1.3 to 1.5 centimeters to either  side of the midline  specially designed in number 17  ventricular needles insulated except for  the distal centimeter are inserted into  the ventricles and air is injected  if the ventricular needles have pierced  the cingulum appropriately they are  withdrawn until the bare ends lie in the  cingulate region and then the radiation  is carried out may i add the next slide  please  initially the lesions replaced 2.5  centimeters posterior to the anterior  tips of the frontal horns and you can  see the  needles in the cingulum in this slide  this is according to the method of  faults in white experience and a  suggestion from paul yakovlev  a very famous  neuropathologist has induced us to  select a target four centimeters back of  the tips of the frontal horns in our  more recent operations  using a radio frequency lesion generator  a current of 8 watts is applied to the  electrodes for 60 seconds  two contiguous lesions are placed  vertically in each cingulum may i have  the next slide please  this is a whole brain section stained by  the lawyers method it has been taken at  about the locus of our lesions and is  presented for orientation purposes the  shaded areas show the area of the  cingulate destruction may i have the  next slide please  this is an example of the destruction  produced  this brain section comes from a man with  intractable pain who died from his lung  cancer note the relatively large size of  the lesions  we believe that adequate destruction is  vital to surgical success  ten cancer patients with severe pain  have been subjected to cingulotomy  adequate pain relief was obtained in  seven  for periods up to three months  our main purpose however has been to  investigate cingulotomy as a method of  treating the mentally ill  and the remainder of my remarks will be  concerned with 40 psychiatric patients  who have been followed three months to  four years after their last operation  let me  have the next slide please  these are the major symptoms of the 40  psychiatric patients 34  are thought to have had a severe mood  disturbance of the manic depressive type  and six the diagnosis is in doubt  all of the group have been treated with  talk and medicine  39 of the 40 had pre-operative  electroshock therapy  note also  that 35 of the 40 were totally disabled  that's the second line in that  slide  these patients represent a refractory  five percent of the total patient load  at the hospital which had been treating  them  now the next slide please  this chart shows the age at onset and at  operation  we have been dealing mostly with a  younger group of patients many of whom  might have been called schizophrenic by  other psychiatrists and all of whom  a difficult treatment problem our  youngest patient was 15  the oldest 80.  the average preoperative duration of  illness was about 10 years now i have  the next slide please  now these are our results the eight  patients called well  are currently symptom-free the markedly  improved group are functioning  effectively in society although  psychiatric supervision and some form of  treatment are still necessary  the improved patients are still disabled  but at the time of the presentation of  this paper they do not require  hospitalization  the slightly improved and unimproved  require no further categorical  delineation we might try to excuse this  lack of useful improvement on the  grounds of errors in psychiatric  diagnosis  improper lesion placement inadequate  lesion size and so forth but i will uh  resist this temptation  grab the next slide please  this is a comparison of the results of  surgery for mental illness is recorded  by different observers scrutinizing  different operations  the degree of improvement  is about the same in all three groups  and is surprisingly similar to that  reported by agus moniz 30 years ago  in his first article published in 1936  moniz stated that 14 of 20 psychiatric  patients subjected to frontal lobotomy  were better post operatively and that  five of the six failures occurred in  patients suffering from schizophrenia  in one respect however and i refer you  to the last column  in one respect however the cingulotomies  and cingulectomies are different from  the leukotomy series the operation seems  to be much safer  now a word about  multiple operations  10 of the 40 psychiatric patients have  had more than one operation and one  patient has had seven  his iq remains about 130.  in this latter respect i want to make it  clear that i do not believe that  cingulotomy can turn a [ __ ] into a  genius but it is true that if nine  patients who had cortical function tests  before and after operation  none was worse post-operatively and six  had slightly higher scores  may i have the next slide please  this short bibliography has two virtues  the articles listed are excellent in  themselves and they also contain copious  additional references for the serious  student of the subject of operative  attempts to influence the course of  mental illness  in conclusion then  we have carried out 85 bilateral  singulotomies on 61 patients  10 of these operations have been for  pain and we have been able to confirm  the findings of faults in white  that cingulotomy is a value in  controlling otherwise intractable pain  75 of these operations have been done  for mental illness primarily those  conditions characterized by profound and  chronic  of affect  30 of 40 patients followed three months  to four years after operation have shown  useful improvement  these good results could be due to  chance to the so-called non-specific  effects of surgery or to other factors  than surgical intervention  naturally we prefer to think that there  is a specific operative effect but we  have no concrete proof of this  the operation does seem to be safe  however and this despite the fact that  our lesions have been large  we believe that it is necessary to  destroy the most medial cingulate fibers  bilaterally and have re-operated  patients to be certain of this  destruction as well as to enlarge the  lesion  finally and most importantly i as a  neurosurgeon do not believe that this  operation is a panacea for mental  illness not even for effective disorders  such as manic depressive disease or  psychosis  cingulotomy should be considered an  incident in the total treatment of the  severely disabled patient and any  neurosurgeon who performs it will be  disappointed in his results unless he is  able to work with psychiatrists who will  treat these patients intensively before  and after surgery with all of the  chemical and physical agents available  for the biological approach to the  therapy of mental illness  you

